Learning GitHub Actions – Day 12: Pull Request Event
Continuing Phase 2: Workflow Configuration in my GitHub Actions learning journey!

📌 What is the pull_request Event?
The pull_request event is a GitHub Actions trigger that automatically starts a workflow when a pull request activity occurs in a repository.
It is commonly used to validate code changes before merging them into the target branch.
For example, when a developer creates a pull request to merge changes from a develop branch into main, GitHub Actions can automatically run build and test workflows.

🛠️ Practical Example:
name: Pull Request Demo
  on:
   pull_request:
     branches:
       - main

  jobs:
   test:
     runs-on: ubuntu-latest
     steps:
       - name: Checkout Repository
         uses: actions/checkout@v4

        - name: Display Message
         run: echo "Pull request detected!"

        - name: Run Tests
         run: echo "Running application tests..."

🔍 How it works:
pull_request: Triggers the workflow when a pull request event occurs.
branches: Specifies the target branches for pull requests.
main: Runs the workflow for pull requests targeting the main branch.
actions/checkout: Checks out the repository code.
run: Executes the specified commands.

💡 Key Takeaway:
The pull_request event helps automate code validation before merging changes, improving code quality and reducing the chances of introducing errors into the main branch.
